meta {
  name: Decide Request - rejects WITHDRAWN
  type: http
  seq: 19
}

patch {
  url: {{apiUrl}}/sessions/{{sessionId}}/requests/{{playerId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "status": "WITHDRAWN"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 400
  res.body.success: eq false
  res.body.error.code: eq REQUEST_VALIDATION_FAILED
}

docs {
  Regression check (code review finding, 2026-10-23): `DecideRequestDto` used to validate
  `status` with `@IsEnum(RequestStatus)` -- the whole shared enum, including `WITHDRAWN`.
  Since `DecideRequestUseCase` only special-cased `REJECTED` and treated everything else as
  an approval (`if (status === REJECTED) reject(); else approve()`), sending
  `{"status":"WITHDRAWN"}` to this host-only decide route used to **silently approve** the
  request instead of being rejected -- consuming a slot on input the host never actually
  asked to approve.

  Fixed by narrowing the DTO to `@IsIn([PENDING, APPROVED, REJECTED])`, the only three
  values this route is ever meant to accept (`PENDING` is the undo signal
  `SessionController` dispatches to `UndoDecisionUseCase`). `WITHDRAWN` is only ever valid
  on the requester's own `PATCH .../requests/mine/withdraw`. `DecideRequestUseCase` also
  gained an explicit `input.status !== APPROVED` check (was an implicit `else`) as a second
  layer, in case a future status value reaches the use case some other way.

  Runs against the shared request regardless of its current actual status -- DTO
  validation happens before the use case ever reads it.
}
